Number of subsequences of size k from an array of size n is nCk.

Abstract: nCk and nPk

nCk (Combinations):
The number of ways to choose k items from n total items when order does not matter.
Often read as "n choose k" and written as C(n, k) or nCk.

nPk (Permutations):
The number of ways to choose and arrange k items from n total items when order does matter.
Written as P(n, k) or nPk.


Important fixed rules to remember

For distributing identical objects:
Condition                                           Formula
n children, m identical objects, child can get 0+   C(n + m - 1, m)
Every child gets at least 1                         C(m - 1, n - 1)


Permutation Rules:
n distinct elements -> n!

m elements must follow ONE specific relative order (not necessarily adjacent):
-> n! / m!

m elements can have k allowed relative orders:
-> k * n! / m!

m elements must stay TOGETHER in one specific order:
-> (n - m + 1)!

m elements must stay TOGETHER with k allowed internal orders:
-> k * (n - m + 1)!

Key:
Relative order -> divide by m!
Together -> treat as ONE block

to get how many times did any element appeared in nCr you do (n-1)C(r-1)

sum of all non-empty combinations sum(nCr,  r=1...n) = 2^n -1


